 really be using a  hot coal coated with acid  but let's keep things simple and use a  grape  earth needs no introduction and it's  always a pleasure to look at pictures of  our home planet taken from space  interestingly earth and venus are  essentially twins when it comes to size  and mass  they're both made mainly of rock and  iron  so for our model  we'll use a grape for the earth  now as we'll see  for most exoplanets  all we know about the planet is its  diameter and mass which means that venus  and earth which are in reality two very  different worlds would appear to be  identical if they were both orbiting  some distant star  that's a limitation of our knowledge  about exoplanets that's worth  remembering  next is mars the red planet  have you ever seen it in the sky when it  makes its closest approach to the earth  it's fun to watch that red point of  light move through the constellations as  the weeks go by  up close our spacecraft and rovers have  explored the surface and revealed  landscapes of orange brown rocks  mountains and dried up river valleys  it's another rocky planet like venus and  earth though it's a bit smaller  in our scale model  mars is going to be a cranberry  continuing outward the next planet is  jupiter a behemoth made mainly of  hydrogen and helium gas  the gases rotate around in distinct  bands of latitude  occasionally getting swirled up into  storms most famously the great red spot  to be on the same scale as our sun and  the inner four planets jupiter is going  to be nearly seven inches across  size of a cantaloupe  but remember there's no solid surface on  jupiter really we should be using a  self-gravitating ball of gas  but i couldn't find one at the grocery  store  around other stars we found lots of  gaseous exoplanets as large as jupiter  and even larger in fact as we'll see  we've found these giant planets in the  strangest places where we didn't expect  them  that brings us to saturn another gas  giant but this one with a dazzling set  of rings going around the planet  nasa's cassini spacecraft is currently  orbiting saturn delivering breathtaking  views of those rings  someday i hope we'll be able to detect  rings around exoplanets too  but we don't have any way of sending  spacecraft to exoplanets and making  these sorts of detailed images for the  moment we have to be content with  knowing the sizes of exoplanets their  masses the shapes of their orbits  and some characteristics of their  atmospheres as we'll see  coming back to our scale model of the  solar system saturn is going to be a  grapefruit a grapefruit with rings  and finally we have the planets uranus  and neptune  these are sort of in between the rocky  and gaseous planets they both have  substantial amounts of solid material  rock and iron but they also have lots of  hydrogen and helium as well as other  lightweight molecules such as water  methane and ammonia  and they don't have solid surfaces  either instead if you could travel  inside them you'd first find yourself  surrounded by gas  then as you descend and the pressure  increases the atmosphere will become  more like a liquid which gets denser and  denser and gradually becomes solid  uranus and neptune are nearly twins too  when it comes to size and mass  so the solar system has two sets of  twins  but instead of being a pair of grapes  like earth and venus uranus and neptune  are bigger  in our model they're plums  later in this course we'll see that the  most common type of exoplanet known  today has a size in between that of  earth and neptune  these are the so-called super earths or  mini neptunes  okay  now we've got all our planets on the  right scale  sun is as big as i am  and the planets are a pomegranate seed  two grapes cranberry cantaloupe a  grapefruit and two plums  so  where are we gonna put all this fruit  we need to space them out so that the  distances between them are on the same  scale as their sizes  here we have a problem  the distances are vast  even after shrinking down the sizes of  their orbits by a factor of 800 million  we're going to need more room than we  have in this studio  a lot more  to help us visualize and comprehend  these distances let's imagine that i'm  taking a walk in a place that might be  familiar to many of you manhattan in new  york city  i'll start in times square with all  these fruits in my backpack  and while i'm walking i'll drop off each  planet at the appropriate distance  first i walk along broadway from 46th to  47th street about one block north that's  where i drop mercury  already you see the solar system is very  spread out  you've got a person-sized sun at one end  of the block  and all the way at the other end is a  pomegranate seed representing mercury  then i keep walking north about three  quarters of the block that's where i  drop one of our two grapes representing  venus  to get to earth's orbit i need to keep  walking until i've gone a total of just  over two and a half blocks  so i'm between 48th and 49th streets now  i can set down the grape representing  our home planet  and when i look back at the sun in times  square  it looks small  i can cover it up with a dime when i  hold it out at arm's length that's the  same size the actual sun looks to us in  our sky  after that pause  i keep walking north until i've gone a  total of four blocks  where i set down our cranberry mars  i'm feeling pretty good at this point  i've laid down half the planets in just  five minutes of walking  but now it's time to get serious  to reach the distance of jupiter's orbit  and unload this heavy cantaloupe i need  to walk almost 10 more blocks  so i pass carnegie hall and i'm just  about to reach central park about six  tenths of a mile from the sun in times  square  but i'm not done  i keep walking straight into the park  until i'm more than a mile from where i  started where i can lay down the  grapefruit saturn  and get a drink at the tavern on the  green  so i'm feeling refreshed and my load is  lighter now  but i'm not even halfway done with my  walk  uranus and neptune are much more remote  to get to uranus's orbit i need to walk  another 1.1 miles to the very northern  end of central park  and neptune might be uranus's twin in  size and mass but it's not right next  door to get to neptune i need to leave  central park and walk up into harlem up  to around 138th street  bringing my total distance to three and  a half miles  that's where i drop the second  plumb and suddenly i'm relieved that  pluto is not a planet because if it were  i would have needed to walk another mile  to get there  all the way up to yankee stadium and i'm  already pretty wiped out  while i'm resting let's reflect on this  exercise  we've imagined a scale model of a solar  system in which the entire earth  is the size of a grape  and the true distance from the earth to  the sun of 93 million miles  maps onto 600 feet in our model  we've seen how spread out the solar  system is  how small the planets are relative to  the sun  and how different the sizes of the  planets are relative to each other  ranging from a pomegranate seed to a  cantaloupe  one thing to keep in mind is that  although we imagined i was walking in a  straight line and putting the planets  down along that line  in reality the planets are all at  different angles in their orbits  so they're usually in what look like  random locations along the circles of  their orbits  it takes a special coincidence for even  three of them to line up and you'd never  see all eight of them right along a  straight line  even to find all the planets in the same  quadrant of the solar system within 90  degrees of each other is rare it happens  once every 120 years on average  and even more importantly  i want you to be aware of three striking  patterns in the solar system which will  be essential to keep in mind as we study  exoplanets  first of all  the planets go around the sun in orbits  that are very nearly circles  mercury's orbit makes a little circle  that's inside of venus's larger circle  which is inside of earth's circle and so  on  what's interesting about this is that  there's no law of physics that says the  orbits must be circles  more generally the laws of physics say  that orbits are elliptical  they can range anywhere from circles to  extremely elongated narrow ovals  so it's remarkable that the orbits of  all eight planets in the solar system  are nearly perfect circles  the second pattern is that all the  circles  are aligned with each other they all lie  flat in a single plane  like different grooves in a record  for those of you like me who are old  enough to remember what a record looks  like  that's why we were able to make our  scale model on flat ground  i didn't need to climb any stairs or  tall buildings or mountains thank  goodness before putting down the planets  and by the way  that single plane is also aligned with  the equator of the sun so all the  planets are revolving in the same way  the sun is rotating  so the solar system seems like a nice  tidy well-aligned system  the third pattern has to do with the  sizes of the planets  all the little planets  mercury venus earth mars are close to  the sun  and all the big planets jupiter saturn  uranus neptune are much farther away  this is unlikely to be a coincidence  it's almost as though the planets were  neatly sorted  so what was it that caused the little  rocky planets to be so much closer in  than the giant gaseous planets  these three patterns have long been  regarded as clues about the formation of  the solar system and by extension the  formation of planets anywhere in the  universe  and like i said earlier one of the  reasons we study exoplanets is that we  want to see if the patterns that exist  in the solar system  the circular well-aligned orbits with  the small planets inside and the large  planets outside  are universal patterns  or just provincial  fine so to prepare to study exoplanets  let's go back to where we left off in  our walk through manhattan and continue  walking to reach the exoplanets  where is the nearest exoplanet  as we'll see later in this course  there's evidence  decent evidence not completely secure  for a small rocky planet around one of  the closest stars to the sun a star  named alpha centauri b  which shines brightly in the southern  hemisphere in the constellation of  centaurus  so now that i've had a chance to rest  let's walk over there  well the problem is alpha centauri b  is very far away in our scale model  i would have to keep walking  all the way across new york and  connecticut  and way beyond in fact to get to alpha  centauri i'd end up walking 32 000 miles  that's a distance 30 percent longer than  the circumference of the entire earth  so you see the distances between planets  in the solar system might be big  but the distances to even the nearest  stars are stupendously greater  even though we shrank everything down by  a factor of 800 million to try and make  things more familiar we still end up  with astronomical distances between  stars so large  that they're hard to comprehend  but let's just imagine i could somehow  walk for 32 000 miles  and arrive at alpha centauri b the very  nearest exoplanet  what would i see when i get there  what i would see is not just one star  but a trio of stars alpha centauri is a  triple star system  with two stars orbiting one another and  a third little star further out that  orbits around both of the bigger stars  the biggest star is alpha centauri a  it's a little bigger than the sun  in our model it would be six foot eleven  so like a professional basketball player  and then there's alpha centauri b  a smaller orange star about five feet  tall and the third distant star proxima  centauri is a cute little red star about  10 inches across  so you see stars can have a wide range  of sizes too  and  around that orange medium-sized star  alpha centauri b  would be a little grape-sized planet  that's the nearest exoplanet for which  we currently have any evidence  now remember in this scale model the  earth was a couple of city blocks away  from the sun  but the amazing thing about alpha  centauri b  is that its planet is much closer  it's only 24 feet away it would fit  right in the studio  so that's our first sighting of  something really different about  exoplanetary systems they can have  planets that are much closer to their  stars  than any of our planets are to the sun  but you might be asking yourself  how can we possibly know anything about  planets in the alpha centauri system  all our planets are packed into  manhattan  and to get to the nearest exoplanet i  had to travel a distance  more than all the way around the earth  looking back toward the sun  we would be hard pressed to see anything  at all  let alone the little grapes and the  other fruits that we dropped  so how in the world can we actually  study the planets that go around other  stars given how astonishingly far away  even the very nearest stars are from the  sun  well stay tuned that's exactly what  we'll discuss in the next two lectures  [Music]  you
 

